      "title": "Calibration and feedback in evidence-driven compilation",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Shared concept",
          "content": "Several sources show compilers using measured or analyzed evidence to steer decisions. The DNA circuit workflow calibrates effective concentrations, identifies outlier gates, tunes output behavior, and uses a synthesis-error-aware model. Profile-guided and machine-learning compiler work similarly use runtime profiles, empirical data, and program features to guide optimization decisions."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Forms of evidence",
          "content": "The evidence differs by domain: DNA compilation uses biochemical calibration and fluorescence behavior, profile-guided C optimization uses execution counts on weighted flow graphs, and machine-learning compilation uses training data and quantifiable program features. A topic can synthesize these as feedback loops that make compiler decisions depend on observed system behavior rather than fixed heuristics alone."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Limits",
          "content": "The sources also warn that evidence quality matters. Machine-learning optimization is limited by training-data representativeness, while unpurified seesaw circuits remain sensitive to synthesis, stoichiometry, and leak-reaction concerns even after calibration."
        }
